B=[
body(0,"Most of us came up through a Boolean education. A bit is one or zero. A program runs or it does not. A test passes or fails. Even uncertainty often arrives as a small wobble around a yes-or-no answer.","B00_Boolean","A one tile and zero tile flank a pass-fail switch; a narrow error band wobbles without changing the bit."),
body(1,"Probability is a different animal. Its output is a degree of belief: a number between zero and one that says how confident you should be, given what you currently know.","B01_Degree","The bit opens into a zero-to-one belief bar and a marker settles between its endpoints."),
body(2,"Two engineers can see exactly the same evidence, assign different probabilities, and both reason correctly. They arrived with different priors. Probability is conditional on an information state.","B02_TwoEngineers","Two engineer pegs face one evidence card; distinct prior tiles feed distinct belief bars."),
body(3,"That shift should bother you a little. Boolean instinct works in deterministic systems. It misleads when you work from a model rather than the world, and every machine learning system, AI deployment, and statistical inference is a model.","B03_ModelWorld","A dark model block casts a paper silhouette toward a separate world block; a bit cannot bridge the gap."),
body(4,"A sample space, omega, is every possible outcome. For a die it is one through six. An event is any subset. The event even lifts the tiles two, four, and six from the omega tray.","B04_SampleSpace","An omega tray holds six numbered outcome tiles; two, four, and six lift as one event."),
body(5,"The first axiom is nonnegativity. P of A is at least zero for every event A. A probability bar can begin at the floor, but it never extends below zero.","B05_Nonnegative","An event tile drives a belief bar down to its zero floor, where a stop catches it."),
body(6,"The second axiom is normalization. P of omega equals one. Something has to happen. Sick or healthy exhausts the patient's sample space, so together their bars fill the whole height.","B06_Normalization","A patient peg faces sick and healthy tiles whose two stacked bars fill a single one-height frame."),
body(7,"The third axiom is additivity. For disjoint A and B, P of A union B equals P of A plus P of B. Even and five cannot both occur on one die roll, so their bars stack without overlap.","B07_Additivity","Even tiles and the five tile travel in separate lanes, then their bars stack into one union bar."),
body(8,"Those three rules are the entire foundation. Conditional probability, Bayes' theorem, and the central limit theorem are derived from them. When a formula becomes mysterious, return to the three supports.","B08_Foundation","Three labeled pillars support a platform carrying conditional probability, Bayes, and central limit theorem tiles."),
body(9,"Two consequences follow. P of the empty set is zero: nothing happening has probability zero. And P of A complement is one minus P of A.","B09_Consequences","An empty tray lands at zero; beside it, one full bar splits into A and its complement."),
body(10,"For the die, the complement of even is the three outcomes left behind: one, three, and five. The event and its complement partition the omega tray and fill the same total of one.","B10_Complement","Even tiles lift from omega, leaving one, three, and five; the two groups fill a complete frame."),
body(11,"Every probability calculation in this book follows from these rules. The result is not a bit. It is your degree of belief given what you know, which is why two careful engineers can differ.","B11_Close","The three axiom pillars turn back into two engineers with different markers on one zero-to-one bar."),
]
